const cineQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'You move a subject from 1 metre to 2 metres from a lamp. The light on them falls to:',
      choices: [
        'Half',
        'A quarter — the inverse-square law: intensity falls with the square of distance',
        'The same — light travels infinitely',
        'Twice',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Double the distance, quarter the intensity (2² = 4×). This one law drives on-set behaviour: subjects choreographed to stay near the key hold exposure, moving away drops them into darkness — which is why a character walking out of a lamplight pool can "act out" of the shot, on purpose.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Hard light versus soft light is decided by:',
      choices: [
        'The wattage of the lamp',
        'The apparent SIZE of the source from the subject — a tiny bare bulb casts crisp shadows, a 12-foot silk (or a cloudy sky) wraps the subject in gradual shadow',
        'The colour of the light',
        'The camera settings',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Shadow edge quality tracks source size relative to distance: small and near = hard (specular, dramatic, noir), large = soft (wrap-around, flattering, memory-like). This is why the sun is a soft source on overcast days and a hard one at noon — same star, different apparent size.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A thriller lights an interrogation room with a single hard key and almost no fill. The high key-to-fill ratio is:',
      choices: [
        'A technical mistake — fill should always balance the key',
        'A deliberate low-key style: deep blacks and high contrast signal danger and moral murk — the ratio is the genre signal, read before dialogue',
        'Only for black-and-white films',
        'High-key lighting',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Key-to-fill ratio is tone: 2:1 flatters (comedy, romance), 8:1 and beyond interrogates (noir, horror, thrillers). Gordon Willis underexposing The Godfather earned him the nickname "Prince of Darkness" and changed studio standards — the ratio is authorship, not error.',
    },
    {
      q: '"Motivated lighting" means:',
      choices: [
        'Lights aimed at the actors\' motivation',
        'Every source reads as plausible within the world — a window, a lamp, a neon sign — even when three hidden fixtures actually build it',
        'Coloured gels',
        'Using only sunlight',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The audience accepts light that could exist: the classic scheme hides its artistry behind plausibility (the "practical" lamp glowing in frame while off-screen units do the work). Breaking motivation openly is also a style — musicals and horror announce their unreality on purpose.',
    },
  ],
};

